    dict(key='27-class-1', kind='code', title='NaiveStore — checks written one by one',
         body='// NaiveStore: the first version: a check in each route\n.add("GET", "/orders/{id}", signedIn(Handlers::orderDetails))\n...\n.add("GET", "/admin/users", call -> isAdmin(call) ? Handlers.listUsers(call) : forbidden())\n...\n.add("DELETE", "/admin/users/{name}", Handlers::deleteUser)',
         narration='The naive store wraps each handler in its own check. [[slnc 300]] Reading an order only asks: is someone signed in? [[slnc 300]] Listing users asks for the admin role. [[slnc 300]] But deleting a user, added later, has no check at all.'),
    dict(key='28-class-2', kind='code', title='Policy — no rule means no',
         body='// Policy: every rule in one table, and one decision\nRule rule = rules.get(route);\nString who = user.map(User::name).orElse("someone signed out");\nif (rule == null) {\n    return deny(who, route, 403, "no rule for this route, so it is refused");\n}',
         narration='The policy looks up the rule for the route. [[slnc 300]] No rule means no: four oh three. [[slnc 300]] That is deny by default, in four lines.'),
    dict(key='29-class-3', kind='code', title='Policy — the owner check answers 404',
         body='// Policy: every rule in one table, and one decision\nif (rule.ownerAllowed()) {\n    Optional<Order> order = data.order(params.get("id"));\n    if (order.isPresent() && order.get().owner().equals(user.get().name())) {\n        return Decision.ALLOW;\n    }\n    return deny(who, route, 404, "no order " + params.get("id") + " that belongs to " + who);',
         narration='For an order, the policy looks up who owns it. [[slnc 300]] The owner is allowed. [[slnc 300]] Anyone else gets four oh four, the same answer as a missing order. [[slnc 300]] And the refusal is logged, with a name, never a token.'),
    dict(key='30-class-4', kind='code', title='GuardedStore — one choke point',
         body='// GuardedStore: the fixed store: the policy first, then the handler\nPolicy.Decision decision = policy.decide(m.route(), m.params(), call.user(), data);\nreturn decision.allowed() ? m.handler().handle(call) : Reply.status(decision.status(), decision.reason());',
         narration='The guarded store asks the policy first, for every request. [[slnc 300]] Only an allowed request reaches the handler. [[slnc 300]] The handlers themselves have no checks at all.'),
    dict(key='31-class-5', kind='code', title='GuardedStore — the route inventory',
         body='// GuardedStore: the fixed store: the policy first, then the handler\nList<Route> routesWithoutRules() {\n    return table.routes().stream().filter(r -> policy.rule(r).isEmpty()).toList();',
         narration='And this is what the inventory test calls. [[slnc 300]] It lists every route that has no rule. [[slnc 300]] The test fails while the list is not empty.'),
    dict(key='32-outro', kind='outro', title='Thanks for Watching',
         body=['Full source, notes, diagrams and an animated walkthrough', 'are in the repository. Add a route,', 'and watch the inventory test fail.'],
         narration="That's broken access control. [[slnc 400]] If you remember one sentence, make it this one. [[slnc 300]] Signed in is not the same as allowed, so check every request, at one place, and refuse whatever has no rule. [[slnc 500]] The full source code, written notes, diagrams, and an animated walkthrough are all in the repository. [[slnc 300]] It runs on your own machine, with nothing installed except a Java development kit. [[slnc 500]] Here is one exercise to try. [[slnc 300]] Add a new route, and watch the inventory test fail until it has a rule. [[slnc 500]] If you found this useful, a like really helps other people discover it. [[slnc 300]] Kindly subscribe to the channel. [[slnc 300]] It helps the channel grow and bring you more content. [[slnc 400]] Thanks for watching!"),
